-- -*- mode: lua -*-

-- MIDI map for the Hercules DJ Control. Albert Gräf <agggraef@gmail.com>, 2024

--[[

   This is preliminary, to complement the hard-wired controls with some
   essential or at least convenient functions. This comment section here also
   serves as the missing manual for the DJ Control support in Raptor, while
   proper documentation still needs to be written.

   As with the other controller mappings, you can use this by loading the
   djcontrol.map file in Raptor and making sure that your DJ Control device is
   hooked up to Pd's second MIDI input port. If you want to enjoy the
   blinkenlights (see FEEDBACK below), you need to hook it up to Pd's second
   MIDI *output* port as well. This isn't just for the light show (although
   that certainly is a cool byproduct), it also gives you valuable information
   about Raptor's state without having to stare at the computer screen, which
   is always nice in a live performance.

   NOTES:

   The present implementation only supports (up to) two decks. This should
   hopefully suffice for the present DJ controller series by Hercules, as even
   their flagship controllers have no more than 2 channels, AFAICT.

   IMPORTANT: To enable the deck-specific features, you need to assign a deck
   number to each raptor instance, which can be done in the init subpatch, or
   by sending the init subpatch a corresponding 'deck' message in an ensemble
   patch (see raptors2.pd for an example). The possible 'deck' values are -1
   (decks are disabled, only the global controls are available), 0 ("omni"
   mode, all decks send to all raptor instances, as with a regular control
   surface), 1 and 2 (assigns the raptor instance to the left or right deck,
   respectively).

   Also note that the available modes and number of pads vary with the
   particular device version. Most Hercules DJ Control devices should give you
   at least two mode buttons and four pads, which is what the Raptor
   implementation was designed to work with. (The smallest Hercules devices
   like the "Starlight" and the "Mix" have a very limited set of buttons, so
   you'll have to live with some compromises when using those, and adjust your
   workflow accordingly.)

   Unfortunately, the labels of some of the buttons are also inconsistent
   between the various devices, especially in the loop section. We brush all
   this aside and simply use LOOP as a shorthand for the shifted VINYL key,
   where on the Inpulse 200 MK2 we pretend that the shifted functions are the
   unshifted ones. On the Inpulse 500 and T7 devices, you can also push the
   loop size encoder to get this function. Also note that pad mode 2 is STEMS
   on the Inpulse 200 MK2, whereas it is LOOP on almost any other Hercules
   device that I know of. To avoid any confusion, we refer to mode 2 as
   "mode 2 (LOOP/STEMS)" below.

   The Raptor implementation should hopefully work with all Hercules DJ
   controllers out there. I tested it with the Inpulse 200 MK2 and 500
   devices, which are the only Hercules controllers that I own. Please report
   any bugs or incompatibilites that you find with these or other variations
   of the Hercules controllers. At this time, there are no plans to add
   support for other DJ controllers, but feel free to send me a pull request
   if you do!

   HARD-WIRED CONTROLS:

   These are implemented in Raptor and thus can't be changed using MIDI
   learn. In contrast to the supported other control surfaces, the DJ Control
   implementation in Raptor has a lot of these. It also has a lot of fancy
   performance controls that are not readily available in the Raptor GUI or on
   the other control surfaces. Here is what's currently on offer:

   - BROWSER encoder for preset selection
   - volume fader and filter knob (mapped to CC7 = volume and CC8 = balance)
   - cross fade control, implemented by tying into the volume control
   - jog wheels for scrubbing (pos/anacrusis)
   - VINYL (enable/disable scrubbing)
   - SYNC (sync the playback positions of all instances to the time master)
   - CUE (cue to the anacrusis)
   - mode 1 (HOT CUE) pads (cue a given bar of the loop during loop playback)
   - mode 2 (LOOP/STEMS) pads (select Raptor instances a.k.a. ccmaster)

   Note that Raptor is MIDI-only, so the cross fader, volume, and balance
   controls are effectively 7 bit and rely on what your synthesizer offers;
   don't expect any miracles. Also note that the cross fader will only be
   active in raptor instances with a deck number (1 or 2), otherwise it can be
   remapped using MIDI learn.

   The HOT CUE pads have two different functions depending on whether they are
   used while holding the shift key. When shifted, the pads relocate to the
   beginning of the bar immediately, otherwise they do a smooth transition
   without changing the position in the bar. These controls are only active
   during loop playback, and can be remapped using MIDI learn otherwise.

   TRANSPORT (PLAY/PAUSE, CUE, SYNC):

   Raptor is rather unusual as a DJ software. Because it deals with MIDI,
   bars, and beats rather than audio tracks, there are some notable
   differences in how the transport controls and the jog wheels work.

   - First and foremost, in Raptor playback is *always* quantized to beats and
     synchronized to the time master, which can either be a single Raptor
     instance or an external time source (MIDI clock).

   - Second, there's no way to set any real "cues", because there is no track
     to begin with; just the arpeggiator doing its thing or playing back a
     (usually short) loop. The only implicit cue in Raptor is the anacrusis
     (the position in the bar where playback starts).

   - Last but not least, since all playback is always quantized to beats and
     synchronized to the time master, the usual tempo sync isn't needed in
     Raptor. When using the jog wheels, though, the different decks will of
     course go out of phase, and this is where sync becomes useful.

   With these points in mind, here's how the DJ Control's transport controls
   actually work in conjunction with Raptor:

   - CUE moves the playback position to the anacrusis, while SHIFT+CUE sets
     the anacrusis to the current playback position. This works separately for
     each deck, and works during playback and when transport is stopped. When
     transport is rolling, CUE goes to the anacrusis immediately (at the next
     beat), *without* stopping playback.

   - SYNC syncs the playback position of all Raptor instances to that of the
     time master, without changing the playback position of the master. It
     also resets the anacrusis of all instances to 0. SHIFT+SYNC works in a
     similar fashion, but also resets the playback position to 0 (beginning of
     the bar and loop) immediately (at the next beat), on all instances. It
     doesn't matter on which deck you press SYNC, it will always sync all
     instances to the time master.

   - PLAY/PAUSE, you guessed it, starts and stops playback. If transport is
     already rolling, and you press PLAY/PAUSE on the other deck (with the
     button unlit), it switches the time master to that deck without
     disrupting playback. (Note, however, that if the decks have different
     tempo settings, the tempo will change when switching the time master.)

   JOG WHEELS:

   The first thing to note here is that Raptor doesn't actually have a "CDJ"
   mode; if you want to speed things up or slow them down, you'll have to use
   the tempo control instead.

   Therefore the VINYL control merely determines whether the jog wheels are
   active (and scrubbing enabled) at all during playback. (As usual, they are
   always on when transport is stopped.) By default, the jog wheels are on
   during playback, but you can turn them off with the VINYL buttons, so that
   you don't inadvertently move the playback position by hitting a jog wheel
   during a hectic performance.

   Other than that, the jog wheels work pretty much like you'd expect them to.
   That is, touching the top of the jog wheel temporarily suspends playback,
   and moving the jog wheel from ring or top starts scrubbing, which changes
   the playback position. In normal playback, or when transport is stopped,
   this sets the playback position and the anacrusis, thus movements are
   confined to a single bar. If a loop is currently playing, it adjusts the
   playback position within the loop instead. In any case, the jog wheel acts
   as a bipolar control; if it goes negative, that means that the position is
   relative to the *end* of the bar or loop (-1 denotes the last beat, -2 the
   second-last, etc.).

   A final note: You can change the responsiveness of the jog wheels with the
   djcontrol_scrub_factor variable in raptor.pd_lua if needed. Please check
   the source for further details.

   FEEDBACK:

   This is also hard-wired, and only works if you also connect the DJ Control
   device to Pd's second MIDI *output*. It gives some feedback about playback
   (lighting up the PLAY button on the deck which is the time master), mute
   status (on the PFL a.k.a. headphone buttons in the mixer section), loop
   playback status (on the loop IN/OUT buttons), and vinyl a.k.a. scrubbing
   status (on the VINYL buttons). If you have mode 2 enabled, you'll also see
   the currently selected Raptor instance (if any) highlighted on the pads.

   Also, on the bigger devices like the Inpulse 500 which have backlighted
   deck numbers, the big "1" or "2" will light up while you touch the
   corresponding jog wheel (if scrubbing is enabled).

   Last, but not least, the backlight of the big encoder pulses in different
   colors following the rhythm, as determined by the actual pulse weights and
   velocities produced by the arpeggiator.

   NOTE: The encoder backlight could get rather busy with complex meters if
   we'd simply trigger all pulses, so we only do the n most salient pulses, as
   determined by the pulse weights, where the value n is currently hard-coded.
   I found that a value of 7 seems to work best with most common meters. YMMV,
   so you can change that value with the djcontrol_n_pulses variable near the
   beginning of raptor.pd_lua. Again, please check the source for further
   details.

   MAPPED CONTROLS:

   These *can* be edited below or modified using MIDI learn. The DJ Control
   doesn't have nearly enough knobs and faders for mapping all Raptor panel
   parameters, so we don't even try and focus on the most essential
   performance controls instead. Note that there are actually *two* bindings
   for most of these, one for each deck. The only singleton bindings are for
   the global controls in the center section of the device. Here is a brief
   rundown of the current bindings:

   - PLAY/PAUSE (playback toggles, time subpatch)
   - PFL buttons (mute toggles, panel)
   - MASTER button (global mute toggle, sends to all Raptor instances)
   - BROWSER button (global latch toggle, sends to all Raptor instances)
   - LOOP a.k.a. SHIFT+VINYL (loop toggles, panel)
   - IN and OUT (previous/next loop, looper subpatch)
   - LOAD and SHIFT+LOAD (load/save loop file, looper subpatch)
   - TEMPO faders (tempo controls, panel)
   - mode 4 (SAMPLER) pads (mute/latch/bypass toggles, panel)

   Note that the global controls (MASTER and BROWSER buttons), like the
   BROWSER encoder, send to all Raptor instances by default, or to the
   currently selected instance, if any.

   In addition to the global MASTER mute button, the PFL buttons (those with
   the headphone symbols on them) can be used to mute the Raptor instances for
   each deck. Moreover, I also mapped the first 3 pads in mode 4 for each deck
   to the mute, latch, and bypass toggles in the panel. By these means, all
   the voice controls are available on each deck using the pads, while the
   most important of these (latch, mute) also have shortcuts in the mixer
   section of the device, where they can be accessed more easily.

   On the Inpulse 500 and T7, you can also push the encoder above the IN/OUT
   buttons for the LOOP function, and you can change the loop size (in the
   panel) with the encoder itself.

   While you can change all of these bindings, I'd suggest that you keep at
   least the mapping of the PLAY/PAUSE, IN/OUT, and PFL buttons as is, since
   they match up with the hard-wired feedback for these buttons.

]]

{
  [8] = {
    [18] = "tempo",
    [19] = "tempo"
  },
  -- loop size encoder, only available on the Inpulse 500 and T7 models
  [14] = {
    [18] = "loopsize",
    [19] = "loopsize",
  },
  [128] = { [17] = { "latch", true } },
  [130] = { [17] = { "mute", true } },
  [131] = {
    [21] = { "loop", true },
    [22] = { "loop", true }
  },
  [135] = {
    [18] = { "play", true },
    [19] = { "play", true }
  },
  [137] = {
    [18] = { "loop-prev", true },
    [19] = { "loop-prev", true }
  },
  [138] = {
    [18] = { "loop-next", true },
    [19] = { "loop-next", true }
  },
  [140] = {
    [18] = { "mute", true },
    [19] = { "mute", true }
  },
  [141] = {
    [18] = { "loop-load", true },
    [19] = { "loop-load", true },
    [21] = { "loop-save", true },
    [22] = { "loop-save", true }
  },
  -- loop size encoder button, only available on the Inpulse 500 and T7 models
  [172] = {
    [18] = { "loop", true },
    [19] = { "loop", true }
  },
  [176] = {
    [23] = { "mute", true },
    [24] = { "mute", true }
  },
  [177] = {
    [23] = { "latch", true },
    [24] = { "latch", true }
  },
  [178] = {
